harper: Quick flag — the new Next + LangChain sample in examples/next-llm-starter shows two annoying DX quirks in local dev: (1) SSR sometimes gets a 401 on first load because the ephemeral credential isn't present yet, (2) streaming SSE examples keep running in serverless if the client navigates away. Anyone have a lightweight fix we can drop into the sample?

leo: Yep familiar. We call the dev auth service which writes a short-lived cookie; if SSR runs before the cookie is persisted the handler sees no token. How are you wiring the token exchange today?

harper: Current pattern: client hits /api/init to get a cookie, then getServerSideProps requests /api/query which reads req.cookies['rl_token'] and constructs the SDK client. The failure rate is small (~8%) but shows up for new users.

anya: Two practical sample-only mitigations: (A) implement a tiny server-side helper that attempts token read + one fast retry with jitter, only in dev; (B) for streaming, wire an AbortController to the SDK stream and listen for req.on('close') so we cancel model work on client disconnect. Both are small changes and very helpful.

mike: Strong +1 for keeping fixes in examples only. For (A) avoid arbitrary sleeps: do immediate retry with a 30-120ms randomized backoff and log a debug hint. For (B) show the AbortController pattern and comment on edge runtime differences.

harper: Draft snippet I tested for token helper: ```js
async function readDevToken(req){
  if(process.env.NODE_ENV==='production') return req.cookies['rl_token'];
  const first = req.cookies['rl_token'];
  if(first) return first;
  // try one fast retry with small jitter
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.floor(Math.random()*90)+30));
  return req.cookies['rl_token'] || null;
}
```

leo: That's fine for examples but please wrap logs behind a debug flag. Also mention in README: "Why this exists: dev auth proxies sometimes take a tick to persist cookie; prefer server-side token mint when possible."

anya: For streams, here's a compact pattern to recommend in pages/api/stream.js: ```js
const ac = new AbortController();
req.on('close', () => ac.abort());
for await (const chunk of sdk.stream({prompt, signal: ac.signal})){
  res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(chunk)}\n\n`);
  if(res.writableEnded) break;
}
res.end();
``` Note: include a comment that some edge/serverless runtimes surface close differently.

mike: Also add a mock-based unit test for both cases in the eval-harness: simulate cookie missing on first call then present, and simulate req emitter emitting 'close' and assert iterator.return() was called. Deterministic mocks avoid CI flakiness.

harper: Good call. I'll open a tiny PR that: (1) adds examples/shared/dev-token-read.js, (2) updates examples/next-llm-starter API handlers to use it behind NODE_ENV check, (3) updates stream example with AbortController wiring plus a comment, (4) adds two mock-based tests in examples/eval-harness/tests to assert behavior.

leo: One more note: when you add the token helper, include a short comment encouraging production apps to do a server->server token mint handshake instead of relying on dev cookies. Show 3-4 line example for server-side exchange.

anya: Also ensure we don't change prod semantics. The helper must be NODE_ENV !== 'production' only. And logs should be debug-level.

ops-bot: Reminder: examples CI will run for PRs. Make tests mock-only and fast. If you need to skip a test in CI, use --ci markers per repo guidelines.

mike: If SDK support for AbortSignal is not present yet, we should still show a shim in the example that calls iterator.return() when ac.abort() is fired. But ideally file an SDK ticket to add signal param so the pattern is native.

harper: I'll include both patterns: preferred (signal param) and fallback (call return() on iterator). I'll also open a tracking issue against the SDK for native signal support and link it in the PR description.

leo: Thanks. Tiny docs change request: add a Troubleshooting bullet to the starter README: "SSR 401 in local dev -> clear dev proxy cookies, restart dev server, or use server-side token mint. See examples/shared/dev-token-read.js for a dev-only helper." That saves a lot of early support noise.

anya: Love it. If you want I can review the PR and sign off on tests.

harper: PR will land in a minute: 2850012360-examples-ssr-token-retry-and-stream-abort. Tagging @anya for review. Thanks team! :sparkles:

ops-bot: Note: PR opened and CI queued. If tests fail due to timing, prefer mocking rather than sleeps.
